+\part*{Cognitive realization and Buddhist interpretation}
+
+\section{In what sense is awareness of emptiness the natural state?}
+\label{sec:cognitive-ground}
+
+How would we pick out the luminous ground? We could start with the physical vacuum and ask what appears when the awareness map acts on it. Or we could start with the space of appearances and ask which ray is its ground. The Luminous Ground model (LG) gives both procedures independent meaning. Their agreement is what allows it to identify awareness of emptiness with the natural state.
+
+\subsection{Two ways to pick out one ray}
+\label{subsec:cognitive-ground-selection}
+
+LG uses \emph{r\=upadh\=atu}, the domain of matter, for the physical sector $\mathcal H_\Psi$, and \emph{dharmadh\=atu}, the phenomenal domain, for the appearance sector $\mathcal H_\Phi$. Within the first, the physical vacuum $x=\ket{0}$ represents emptiness. Why should an empty ground be represented by a vector of norm one? The Third Karmapa's \emph{Aspiration of Mah\=amudr\=a} describes mind as empty of its own nature and able to appear in any form \cite[epigraph preceding the Introduction]{Yiannopoulos2020}. The analogy LG draws is with a ground from which determinations are possible. A linear operator applied to the zero vector could only yield zero. R1 singles out the vacuum ray as the unique ground of the physical Hamiltonian and requires $x$ to be cyclic for the specified matter algebra. Acting on $x$ with elements of that algebra gives vectors dense in the chosen physical sector, and $x$ itself belongs to that sector. This is LG's reading of the two directions of ``matter is emptiness; emptiness is matter.''
+
+The appearance sector has its own Hamiltonian and determination algebra. R2 selects a unique ground ray, with unit representative $y=\ket{\Omega_0}$ cyclic for that algebra. LG calls the ground so identified \emph{ultimate luminosity}. It calls the vacuum's image $Ax$ \emph{relative luminosity}, since this description depends on what the awareness map does to $x$. R1 and R2 thus select the two grounds independently of the map. Its image of the first could have missed the second. R3 asserts that $Ax$ is nonzero and lies on the line of $y$. Only then can we choose representatives so that $Ax=ay$ with $a=\|Ax\|>0$. For this $A$, the convention fixes their relative phase and leaves a common phase free (Section~\ref{subsec:ground-plane}). A symmetry commuting with the physical ground Hamiltonian can likewise give $x$ a phase while preserving its ray (Section~\ref{sec:symmetry-average}). Different representatives still describe the same vacuum. Its phase freedom therefore supplies no distinction between sentient beings.
+
+It matters which part of this reasoning is an assumption. LG calls a mathematical result already proved in TAUM or in operator theory an \emph{imported theorem}, as in the case of polar decomposition. A \emph{realization axiom} adds structure, as R3 does by requiring the two rays to agree. A \emph{derived proposition} follows from such data and the mathematical results already established. One example is the uniqueness of the restricted ground orientation in Section~\ref{subsec:ground-plane}. A \emph{model choice} selects among available realizations, as we shall do in specifying which effects count as measurements. A \emph{doctrinal identification} gives a state or relation its cognitive or contemplative meaning. An \emph{open question} records what remains to be selected or derived.
+
+Does the agreement of the ground rays also pair the two ground tests? On the unequal-cell path of Section~\ref{sec:ground-correspondence}, copying a constant function gives a constant function, so $Ax=ay$ holds exactly. The polar isometry instead copies each coarse site to the \emph{normalized} constant on its cell. With cells of sizes one and four, the input partner $r=u^*y$ of the output ground passes the input ground test with probability $9/10$. Matching the ground tests requires $ux=y$ for the compatible unit representatives, condition~(iii) of Proposition~\ref{prop:ground-polar-compatibility}. LG's identification of awareness of emptiness with the natural state already applies to the weak bridge.
+
+\subsection{One ground and its two sector descriptions}
+\label{subsec:cognitive-relational-ground}
+
+The vacuum and luminous ground belong to different spaces. In what sense can they describe one ground? Restrict the awareness map to the vacuum line and remove its positive magnitude $a$. Its orientation $u_0=AP_x/a$ carries $x$ to $y$. The graph of this line map in the common carrier is spanned by $z_+=(x\oplus y)/\sqrt2$. Equal sector weights alone would allow every relative phase in Eq.~\eqref{eq:ground-plane-vectors}. For the specified $A$, the restricted orientation selects the aligned ray $[z_+]$.
+
+LG identifies this ray with the nondual relation between emptiness and luminosity. They are the two aspects of one relational ground. Projecting into the two summands gives $x/\sqrt2$ and $y/\sqrt2$. Normalizing these components recovers the conventional vacuum and luminous ground rays. The total vector has Hilbert norm one. Its Krein square is zero because the two sector weights cancel in the grading. On the unequal-cell path $z_+$ lies exactly on the aligned \emph{restricted} graph. Its squared projection on the anti-aligned graph of the \emph{full} polar map is nevertheless $(1-\sqrt{9/10})/2$, about $2.57\%$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:ground-plane-graph-weights}). This measures how the two graph constructions differ.
+
+The anti-aligned companion $z_-=(x\oplus(-y))/\sqrt2$ has the very same vacuum and luminous ground as its sector rays. The minus sign changes their relative phase. Both vectors have the same energy distribution for the direct sum of the sector Hamiltonians (Section~\ref{subsec:ground-plane}). Both are eigenvectors of that direct sum precisely when the two ground energies coincide. The label $\ket{\Omega_{-1}}$ would denote a hypothetical state below the phenomenal ground. The companion's appearance component still lies on the luminous ground ray.
+
+LG reads the Third and Fourth Yogas through Ratn\=akara\'s\=anti. At the Third Yoga differentiated appearances have ceased while luminosity still appears. At the Fourth, even luminosity ceases to appear as an object \cite[pp.~184--185]{Yiannopoulos2012}\cite[pp.~241, 252--253]{Yiannopoulos2017}. Changing the relative sign in $z_-$ leaves both sector rays in place. It gives no condition on what ceases to appear, so LG assigns the companion no role as a further stage of realization.
+
+\subsection{What the two descriptions let us measure}
+\label{subsec:cognitive-ground-measurement}
+
+LG uses the sector projections to extract aspects of a state. It excludes them from the observable algebra chosen for the cognitive event. If a device did count the two sectors as exclusive outcomes, its probabilities on $z_+$ would be one half each. In LG those halves express the balance between the two descriptions. The physical and phenomenal descriptions refer to one occurrence of seeing.
+
+There is a positive measurement claim as well. For any unit $\psi$ in the active input space $S$, let $V=u|_S$ be the full polar isometry. LG reads the aligned graph ray $[(\psi\oplus V\psi)/\sqrt2]$ as one cognitive event. Its normalized compressions give a physically realized state $[\psi]$ and its phenomenal presentation $[V\psi]$. For an effect $E$ on $S$, the probability of $E$ in $\psi$ equals that of $VEV^*$ in $V\psi$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:matched-born-probabilities}). This is the Born-rule correspondence between the two descriptions. The ground graph just discussed is always available through $u_0$. The general measurement claim specializes to the pair $(x,y)$ precisely under strong compatibility.
+
+\subsection{What remains open about appearance}
+\label{subsec:cognitive-ground-excess}
+
+The ground itself has a physical presentation. Indeed, $y=Ax/a$ lies in $\Ran A$. Any family $\mathcal C$ containing this map therefore has $y\in D_{\mathcal C}$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:joint-presentation-space}. A direction orthogonal to every admitted range must therefore be orthogonal to $y$. Differentiation can also occur within a presentation. The paired space $D$ of $A$ can contain directions orthogonal to $y$. Even for a single map LG leaves open what its defect represents. A vector there might describe an unrealized imagining, a counterfactual or chimerical possibility, or a mathematically admissible direction inaccessible to cognition. To assert a common phenomenal excess, Conjecture~\ref{conj:asymmetric-nonduality} needs independent grounds both for counting a direction as a possible appearance and for treating the admitted physical presentations as exhaustive.
+
+A question about time also remains. LG pairs two conditions in its description of the realized ground. The evolution fixes the ground state $P_y$ and every centered element of the exhausted determination algebra annihilates $y$ (Eq.~\eqref{eq:ground-character} and Section~\ref{sec:asymmetric-nonduality-moments}). The five-site path lets us vary the evolution and the active family independently. Since $H_5y=0$, evolution under $H_5$ fixes $P_y$. Yet the first-site projection $P_1$, if active, takes the constant ground to $e_1/\sqrt5$. Conversely, take $H_5$ alone as the active family. Every centered element of its algebra annihilates $y$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:ground-keeping-family}). A separately applied site pulse $e^{-i\tau P_1}$ changes the phase at the first site alone and moves the ground ray for $0<\tau<2\pi$. LG specifies the two conditions at the endpoint. How an earlier family of determinations could become exhausted remains open.
+
